INSERT [dbo].[ContactUs] ([Id], [Name], [Email], [Subject], [ContactDate]) VALUES (10436, N'Booker Brogan', N'booker.brogan@gmail.com', N'
What we discovered about bank alternatives for your business!



Business Introduction,



Yep, We said it. That’s how we feel about most bank loans. And We can tell you exactly why. In fact, We can give you 9 reasons why (and a better alternative).

Collateral Requirement: Banks often require small businesses to provide collateral, like property or equipment, as security for the loan. If the business fails to repay the loan, they can lose these valuable assets.

Rigorous Application Process: Applying for a bank loan can be time-consuming, with numerous forms, background checks, and financial documents required, which may be daunting for any business owner.

Strict Qualification Criteria: Banks generally have stringent qualification criteria, including credit score checks, that many small businesses might not meet.

Lengthy Approval Time: The time between applying for a loan and receiving the funds can be long, causing delays for businesses in urgent need of capital.

Short Loan Terms: Sometimes banks offer shorter repayment terms, making monthly repayments higher and potentially straining a small business''s cash flow.

Early Repayment Penalties: Some bank loans come with penalties for repaying the loan earlier than the stipulated period, making it costly for businesses that manage to improve their financial position quickly.

Limited Flexibility: Bank loans often come with fixed terms and conditions that may not be suitable for the dynamic nature of small businesses. There''s limited room to adjust repayment schedules or amounts based on fluctuating cash flows.

Debt Burden: Taking on debt can be a significant burden for small businesses. If the business does not generate expected revenues, it can lead to insolvency or severe financial strain.

Personal Liability: In many cases, small business owners might be required to give a personal guarantee for the loan. This means that if the business cannot repay the loan, the owner''s personal assets could be at risk.

SO, what’s better???



An advance on your own money of course.



And, we’re going to share ALL of the information with you. All the good stuff and all the things you should watch out for.



It’s all here in our FREE eBook “Merchant Cash Advances: The Ultimate Guide”. Just subscribe to our email list on our website and you’ll get instant access to read it or download it. PLUS, we’ll keep you in the loop on all the valuable content we share weekly for your business.


www.SterlingLoanAdvocates.com






 

To your business’ limitless potential,

Sterling Loan Advocates.


You may unsubscribe or change your contact details at any time.
Please email us at: admin@sterlingloanadvocates.com
', CAST(N'2023-10-26T17:04:55.033' AS DateTime))
GO
